@@@ section
{"id": "trees", "title": "Trees & Recursion", "summary": "Recursive structures on the heap: grow them, tear them down in the right order, and turn them into strings."}

@@@ problem insert-into-a-bst
@@ meta
{
  "title": "Insert into a Binary Search Tree",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "topics": ["Binary tree", "Recursion", "malloc"],
  "params": [{"name": "root", "type": "tree"}, {"name": "values", "type": "int[]"}],
  "examples": [
    {"input": ["[4,2,7,1,3]", "[5]"], "output": "[4,2,7,1,3,5]", "explanation": "5 > 4 goes right; 5 < 7 goes left; 7 has no left child, so the new node goes there."},
    {"input": ["[]", "[5,3,8]"], "output": "[5,3,8]", "explanation": "Inserting into an empty tree creates the root."}
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Recursive idea: inserting into an empty tree returns a new node; otherwise insert into the correct subtree and store the result back.",
    "root->left = bstInsert(root->left, val) works whether root->left was NULL or not.",
    "Remember to set the new node's left and right to NULL."
  ],
  "constraints": ["0 <= number of nodes <= 1000", "all values are distinct", "values never already exist in the tree"],
  "memory": "Each insertion allocates exactly one node. The harness frees the whole tree at the end."
}
@@ description
Insert `val` into a **binary search tree** (BST) and return the root. In a BST, every node's left subtree holds smaller values and its right subtree holds larger values.

```c
struct TreeNode {
    int val;
    struct TreeNode *left;
    struct TreeNode *right;
};

struct TreeNode *bstInsert(struct TreeNode *root, int val);
```

The harness inserts each number of `values` in order (`root = bstInsert(root, v)`), prints the tree in level order (like LeetCode, `null` marks a missing child), and checks that each insertion allocated exactly one node.

Insert the new node as a leaf: don't rebalance or restructure the tree.
@@ prelude
struct TreeNode {
    int val;
    struct TreeNode *left;
    struct TreeNode *right;
};
struct TreeNode *bstInsert(struct TreeNode *root, int val);
@@ starter
#include <stdlib.h>

/* Provided by the harness:
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     struct TreeNode *left;
 *     struct TreeNode *right;
 * };
 */

struct TreeNode *bstInsert(struct TreeNode *root, int val) {
    // TODO
    return root;
}
@@ solution
#include <stdlib.h>

struct TreeNode *bstInsert(struct TreeNode *root, int val) {
    if (root == NULL) {
        struct TreeNode *node = malloc(sizeof *node);
        if (node == NULL) return NULL;
        node->val = val;
        node->left = node->right = NULL;
        return node;
    }
    if (val < root->val)
        root->left = bstInsert(root->left, val);
    else
        root->right = bstInsert(root->right, val);
    return root;
}
@@ driver
#include "lc_driver.h"
#define LC_WANT_TREE
#include "lc_ds.h"

int main(void)
{
    while (lc_next_case()) {
        struct TreeNode *root = lc_read_tree();
        int n, i;
        int *vals = lc_read_int_array(&n);
        long long before = lc_alloc_count();
        for (i = 0; i < n; i++) root = bstInsert(root, vals[i]);
        if (lc_alloc_count() - before != n)
            lc_fail("%d insertions made %lld allocations. Each insertion should allocate exactly one node.",
                    n, lc_alloc_count() - before);
        lc_out_tree(root);
        lc_free_tree(root, "a node of the tree");
        lc_end_case();
    }
    return lc_finish();
}
